      "evidence": "**Evidence \u2014 Gone:** Collection not terminated.\n\n**Evidence \u2014 High Risk (realized reductions):** The **number interviewed fell to ~6,100 in 2025**, versus **7,700 in 2024** and ~13,000/year in the survey's early years (2003: 20,720; 2004: 13,973). The **response rate hit a record low of 25.7% in 2025**, down from 32.4% (2024) and from 57.8% at inception \u2014 a >55% relative decline. Approximately **6 weeks of 2025 diaries (Sept 30\u2013Nov 11) are permanently missing**, and BLS states it \"is not possible to quantify the effect of the 2025 shutdown on the ATUS estimates.\" BLS also notes the weighting methodology was **not** adjusted for the shutdown gap. Smaller realized samples directly reduce the granularity at which reliable subgroup estimates can be produced.\n\n**Evidence \u2014 Moderate Risk:** The **designed** granularity (400+ activity codes; where/with whom), **timeliness** (on-time June release), and **frequency** (annual) are unchanged. The sample reduction stems from falling response rates (a long-running, structural challenge) plus a one-off shutdown \u2014 not an agency decision to reduce the dataset's detail.\n\n**Evidence \u2014 No Known Issue:** None: quality indicators have clearly deteriorated.\n\n**Most appropriate: High Risk.** I rate this High because reductions have already been *realized* \u2014 the effective sample is roughly half its historical size, the response rate is at an all-time low, and 2025 contains a permanent multi-week gap \u2014 which the rubric places under \"reductions in granularity/timeliness/frequency.\" A defensible alternative is **Moderate Risk** if one reads \"granularity\" strictly as variable/geographic detail (unchanged) rather than statistical precision; I flag both.\n\n---"
