      "level": "No Known Issue",
      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting each level**\n\n- **Gone / High Risk:** No evidence found. I found no reporting or site evidence of prior Census of Agriculture files or data elements being withdrawn. The 2022 cycle is complete (U.S./state/county releases Feb 13, 2024; Puerto Rico, CNMI, USVI, Guam, American Samoa through April 2025). Prior editions remain online at `nass.usda.gov/AgCensus/`, in Quick Stats, and as \"Archived Ag Census Reports\" (2002 and earlier) via a Cornell University Mann Library mirror hosted at `nass.usda.gov/AgCensus/archive/` (mirror header dated Sept 26, 2025, still live).\n- **Moderate Risk (candidate evidence):** Two access routes have been retired rather than the data removed \u2014 the **Census Data Query Tool (CDQT)** is commented out of NASS navigation (superseded by Quick Stats), and NASS **ended state- and region-specific reports** in May 2025 (state-level data still exist in national releases and Quick Stats). Agency-wide, other USDA components have removed web content and datasets since 2025; no Census of Agriculture tables have been implicated that I could find.\n- **No Known Issue:** 2022 and all prior-cycle data remain accessible and unaltered in the primary location and in a third-party (Cornell) mirror.\n\n**Assessment: No Known Issue.** Prior-cycle data are public domain, freely mirrored, and demonstrably present. The residual, unquantifiable risk is \"quiet\" removal of demographic tables (race/ethnicity/gender/\"socially disadvantaged producers\") in the current political environment \u2014 no evidence of it, but it is the one removal vector I cannot fully rule out.\n\n---"
