      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting each level**\n\n- **Gone:** Not applicable.\n- **High Risk (candidate evidence):** NASS *has* reduced granularity, timeliness, and frequency across its portfolio: county estimates and the July Cattle report cancelled (Apr 9, 2024; restored Mar 19, 2025 after congressional pressure); **state/regional reports ended May 2025**; **Mink Survey and Agricultural Labor Survey discontinued Aug 2025**. Measurable quality strain: the **March 2026 Prospective Plantings report had a record-low 37.6% response rate**, and a large January 2026 corn-acreage revision moved futures.\n- **Moderate Risk (candidate evidence):** For the census specifically: NASS states it is \"looking for ways to **streamline the questionnaire** to reduce respondent burden and data collection costs\"; the **SOGI/disability pilot (Farm Producer Study) forms were \"Removed\" from the 2027 ICR**, abandoning a researched content expansion; a **\"Short Form\" appears among the 2027 census instruments**; the 2027 census will be fielded by a much smaller, relocated workforce with four of seven divisions under acting directors; a **2022-census response decline** is documented and NASS is trying to reverse it.\n- **No Known Issue:** The 2022 census expanded content (hemp, precision agriculture, internet access); FY2027 explicitly funds coverage-evaluation (ACES) and mail-list improvements; the five-year frequency and county-level granularity are unchanged on paper.\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** No reduction to the census has occurred yet, but the emerging-risk threshold is comfortably met: declining response propensity, large-scale loss of statistical staff mid-cycle, leadership vacancies, and a stated intent to shorten the instrument. If you score at the **NASS-program** level rather than the census-dataset level, **High Risk** is supportable.\n\n---"
