      "evidence": "**Evidence by risk level**\n\n- **Gone:** No evidence found that BLS has removed wholesale historical files. Archival news releases (e.g., `empsit_11202025.htm`, `empsit_12162025.htm`), archived benchmark articles, Handbook of Methods snapshots, and the long CES time series (1919 for total nonfarm; 1939 monthly for major sectors) remain online.\n- **High Risk:** No public file-level removals located. The closest analogue is **confidential microdata/researcher access**: BLS **suspended researcher access to confidential data in the FSRDC network** in 2025 (per the American Statistical Association's *Nation's Data at Risk* report). If one treats the *complete histories* of the \u2248900 discontinued series as \"files removed,\" this level is arguable.\n- **Moderate Risk:** Documented removal of **data elements/series from the primary source**:\n  - With the January 2026 release, the CES State & Area program \"**discontinue[d] approximately 900 employment and hours and earnings series**\" for detailed industries; BLS states \"These series will no longer be available in the BLS database at that time.\" BLS lists them in published tables/XLSX as a mitigation.\n  - The February 2025 national benchmark discontinued or merged series where sample was insufficient.\n  - The 2025 MSA redelineation process dropped **New England City and Town Areas (NECTAs)** and all nonstandard areas except New York City from publication, and dropped some MSAs \u2014 a permanent narrowing of the published geography.\n- **No Known Issue:** Everything else \u2014 long CES-N histories, discontinued-database pages, SIC-era series retrieval, benchmark archives, methodology archives \u2014 remains accessible with no known alteration.\n\n**Most appropriate: Moderate Risk.** Historical *files* look intact, but the \"dataset\" as a published object has genuinely shrunk at the series level, twice in two years (Feb 2025 national; Jan 2026 state/metro), and BLS explicitly says the removed series will no longer be in its database. Anyone reconstructing a long panel of state/metro detailed-industry series will now find gaps that did not exist in 2024. Preserve the discontinued-series lists now (they are the only BLS-published record of what vanished), and prefer third-party vintage mirrors (ALFRED/FRED) for series that are still retrievable there.\n\n---"
