      "evidence": "**Evidence by risk level**\n\n- **Gone:** No \u2014 no proposal to terminate CES; BLS continues to release it monthly.\n- **High Risk:** Multiple \"significant changes in accordance with Administration priorities,\" including **Presidential action directed at this specific dataset**:\n  - **Aug 1, 2025:** presidential firing of the Commissioner over the jobs report \u2014 a direct political intervention in the program's governance.\n  - **FY2026 budget (May 2025):** proposed **reorganizing BLS under the Department of Commerce** (with Census and BEA) \"under the policy direction of the Under Secretary for Economic Affairs,\" and directing BLS to \"refocus efforts on producing data from statistical programs that are Principal Federal Economic Indicators (PFEIs), required by statute, or in use in current law,\" with savings \"from staff attrition and statistical agency reorganization.\"\n  - **Aug\u2013Sep 2025:** nomination (later withdrawn) of a commissioner who had publicly called BLS data a \"random number generator\" and endorsed moving the monthly jobs report to a **quarterly** cadence.\n  - **DoL-directed review** of BLS's dissemination practices (\"not sufficiently focused\"), plus BLS's own **Nov 2024 \"Report on Procedures and Practices for the Equitable and Timely Provision of Data to the Public.\"**\n  - **Governance/access changes:** BLS technical and data-user advisory committees and **FESAC disbanded**; **FSRDC researcher access suspended**; CPI sample reduced; many PPI estimates discontinued; JOLTS state-level monthly data moved to annual (July 2026). These demonstrate both capability and willingness to shrink outputs.\n- **Moderate Risk:** Countervailing evidence specific to CES: **no CES-specific ICR change**, no negative policy note on bls.gov, the Feb 2026 benchmark preserved the national series structure, the monthly release has continued on schedule through 2026, and the new Commissioner is a BLS career economist who testified to improving data quality. One could read all of the above as \"proposed or pending changes\" only.\n- **No Known Issue:** Not supportable.\n\n**Most appropriate: High Risk.** The rubric's High tier includes \"Presidential Action-driven [changes]\u2026 other significant changes in accordance with Administration priorities.\" The firing of the Commissioner was a presidential action aimed squarely at the employment statistics program, and the reorg/refocus directive has already been implemented through product cancellations elsewhere in BLS. I'll flag the counter-argument explicitly: a reviewer who weights **CES-specific** outcomes (monthly release intact; national series structure unchanged in 2026; career-economist Commissioner confirmed) would rate this **Moderate**. My High rating reflects the risk *trajectory*, not the October 2026 steady state.\n\n---"
