      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting each level**\n\n**Gone**\n- No evidence.\n\n**High Risk**\n- The nonfinancial-business retirement is a genuine **reduction in granularity**, and 2022 dropped the 48\u2011month new-car rate. However, these are small and partly offset by added detail (credit-union secured personal loans; 72\u2011month rate).\n\n**Moderate Risk (evidence present)**\n- **Granularity reduced**: one of the release's lender sectors (nonfinancial business) removed in 2025 \u2192 total consumer credit reduced by ~$35B (with a series break added).\n- **Source-data volatility**: finance-company new-car terms switched from Experian's Autocount to **Velocity Risk Report** (April 2024) \u2014 a source change that could affect comparability.\n- **A published data-quality defect occurred**: on **Sept 8, 2025**, DDP flows series were \"calculated for time periods prior to the start of the actual data\"; corrected and republished **Sept 9, 2025** (DDP notice).\n- **Benchmark break**: credit-union nonrevolving levels jumped ~$69B (March 2025) due to a benchmark-formula update, requiring a break.\n- Timeliness/frequency otherwise maintained (monthly, ~5\u2011business\u2011day lag).\n\n**No Known Issue**\n- Frequency (monthly) and timeliness are unchanged; the release continues to add documenting FEDS Notes and series breaks.\n\n**Most appropriate: Moderate Risk.**\nRationale: the release has experienced **element-level granularity reductions and a source-data change**, plus a (corrected) publication defect \u2014 i.e., an emerging/real risk to granularity rather than a loss of frequency or timeliness. Not yet \"High Risk\" (no reduction in frequency or timeliness), and clearly not \"No Known Issue\" given the 2025 sector removal and source switch.\n\n---"
