      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting each level**\n\n**Gone**\n- N/A.\n\n**High Risk**\n- The *publication of the G.19* is not explicitly required by statute (it is a discretionary product), and partial alternative measures exist (the NY Fed Household Debt & Credit Report based on Equifax; the Board's own Z.1 Financial Accounts, which *reuses* G.19 data).\n\n**Moderate Risk (largely fits)**\n- **Not explicitly required by statute**, but the underlying collections are firmly authorized and the release is used to implement the Fed's statutory mission (monetary policy; financial stability):\n  - **FR Act \u00a7 2A (12 U.S.C. \u00a7 225a)** \u2014 dual mandate re: money and credit aggregates;\n  - **FR Act \u00a7 11(a)(2) (12 U.S.C. \u00a7 248(a)(2))** \u2014 authority to require asset/liability reports;\n  - **FR Act \u00a7 12A (12 U.S.C. \u00a7 263)**; **International Banking Act \u00a7 7(c)(2) (12 U.S.C. \u00a7 3105(c)(2))**.\n- No *true* substitute exists for the official G.19 aggregates (Z.1 depends on G.19; private measures differ in scope).\n\n**No Known Issue**\n- Authorization is explicitly named and the content is well specified in the ICRs; clear programmatic use (FOMC/Board monitoring; Z.1 input).\n\n**Most appropriate: Moderate Risk.**\nRationale: neither \"High Risk\" (authorization is *not* vague, and there is clear programmatic use) nor cleanly \"No Known Issue\" (the *release itself* isn't statutorily mandated). It best fits \"not explicitly required by statute but required for implementation of a federal program, and there are no alternative collections that could substitute.\" Note the underlying *collections* are nearer \"No Known Issue.\"\n\n---"
