      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting each level**\n\n**Gone**\n- No evidence (no agency-wide elimination or full defunding; the Fed is self-funded, not appropriated).\n\n**High Risk**\n- **A threshold question**: in **May 2025** Chair Powell directed a system-wide **~10% headcount reduction** (~2,400 of ~24,000), which, if fully realized, would exceed the rubric's \"1,000 or more staff lost\" trigger. Reductions are being pursued via **hiring-freeze alignment, a voluntary deferred-resignation program, and a strategic evaluation** (Powell memo, May 2025; NYT/Reuters/Bloomberg, May 16, 2025; Reserve Bank budget transmittal).\n- Reports of **~30% cuts in the Division of Supervision & Regulation** (from ~500 to ~350) \u2014 but concentrated in *supervision*, not economic statistics (Senate Banking Committee letter, Oct 2025).\n- **Threatened change in leadership**: Chair Powell's term *as Chair* ended **May 2026**; the Administration has publicly pressured and litigated over Fed personnel (e.g., the 2025 attempt to remove Governor Lisa Cook).\n\n**Moderate Risk (most consistent with evidence for the statistics function)**\n- The announced reduction (~10%) sits at the **10\u201339%** band; it is **phased over several years** and **attrition-based**, not an immediate cut.\n- **Budget is not cut 20%**: the Board's 2025 operating budget was **$1,168.6M, +11.3% vs. 2024 actual** (though some items were trimmed post-adoption).\n- No reported 40% cut in the Board's data/statistics functions (Research & Statistics / Office of the Chief Data Officer).\n\n**No Known Issue**\n- Would require <10% staff and <10% budget cuts and no leadership change \u2014 not met.\n\n**Most appropriate: Moderate Risk.**\nRationale: a ~10% planned reduction, larger cuts in one (non-statistical) division, and a threatened leadership change map to \"10\u201339% staff lost\u2026 and/or threatened change in leadership.\" **Caveat:** if the full system-wide ~10% (~2,400) is actually realized, the rubric's \"1,000 or more staff lost\" clause would push this to **High Risk**; today the reduction is announced/phased and concentrated away from the statistics function, so Moderate Risk is the better present read.\n\n---"
