      "evidence": "**Evidence for \"Gone\"** \u2014 No; production continues.\n\n**Evidence for \"High Risk\"** \u2014 Documented reductions in sample granularity, some of them not measured:\n- **Entire collection suspended in 3 of 75 areas** (Lincoln, Provo from April 2025; Buffalo from June 2025) and **~15% of the sample suspended on average in the other 72 areas**, affecting both the Commodities & Services and Housing surveys; \"the number of collected prices and the number of collected rents used to calculate the CPI has temporarily been reduced.\" BLS cautions it \"did not measure the impact of collection suspensions on subnational and item indexes,\" which \"may exhibit more volatility.\" Statistically the headline effect is tiny (<0.01 pt on 12-month inflation in simulation).\n- **Staffing-shortage-driven cuts**: BLS told economists it \"temporarily reduced the number of outlets and quotes it attempted to collect due to a staffing shortage\" in April 2025 (AP); sample reductions were attributed to aligning workload \"with resource levels.\"\n- **Sample size language in the current news release** describes ~\"22,000 retail establishments\" (vs. ~23,000 in prior guidance) \u2014 consistent with reduced collection.\n- **Permanently missing month** (Oct 2025) requiring carry-forward imputation; 2025 annual/semiannual averages and median price-change statistics computed from 11 (or 5/2) values.\n- **More imputation, more secondary/vendor data**: leased cars and trucks switched to purchased transaction data (Apr 2025); wireless telephone services switched to secondary-source data with a \"non-traditional indexing method\" (Jul 2025); long-term care insurance dropped (Oct 2025). These plausibly *improve* accuracy but reduce the transparency of what is directly observed.\n- Declining response rates plus shrinking field capacity raise the risk for sub-aggregate and local indexes.\n\n**Evidence for \"Moderate Risk\"** \u2014 Reductions were framed as temporary; the headline national index, its monthly frequency, and its 23-area/region/division/size-class publication structure remain; a **2028 geographic revision** is proceeding (new sample from the 2020 census; 6 new PSUs in the first wave in 2028, 8 more by 2030) \u2014 evidence of investment in quality rather than deterioration. Methodological changes were published in advance with analysis.\n\n**Evidence for \"No Known Issue\"** \u2014 Not sustainable: granularity was in fact reduced.\n\n**Selected risk level: High Risk.** The reductions are implemented, not hypothetical, they cut the collected sample (granularity), and BLS itself declines to quantify their effect on subnational/item indexes \u2014 exactly the \"reductions in granularity/timeliness/frequency\" test. Frequency and headline timeliness were preserved, which is why this is not \"Gone.\"\n\n---"
