      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting each level**\n\n- **Gone:** No \u2014 collection and publication continue.\n- **High Risk:** (a) **Timeliness has already been reduced** \u2014 2025 reschedulings; (b) **granularity reductions of record**: permanent loss of the **Cotton Objective Yield Survey** (physical measurement in randomly selected fields \u2014 the most intensive verification layer for cotton), cancellation-then-congressionally-forced-restoration of **county crop estimates**, and AFBF's assessment that resource constraints \"continue to affect the **depth, frequency, and geographic granularity** of NASS products\"; (c) **measured accuracy degradation**: the **January 2026 corn acreage revision was unprecedented**, moved futures >5%, triggered an **internal NASS review**, and is characterized by USDA's own Deputy Secretary as something NASS will \"be held accountable for\"; (d) **record-low response**: 37.6% on March 2026 *Prospective Plantings*; (e) NASS's own **RFI summary** reports stakeholder \"frustration over revisions and perceived declines in accuracy\" and concerns about \"response rates, production weighted estimates\"; (f) **ASA**: staff losses \"have severely challenged the agency,\" and the January 2026 episode plus the response-rate record are \"indications that NASS needs more resources.\"\n- **Moderate Risk:** The *Crop Production* report still appears **monthly**, at **8:30 a.m. ET on the 9th\u201312th**, with **national, state, and county** dimensions; county coverage is actively **expanding** (June 2026 notice adds cotton counties); NASS has launched an internal review and a public RFI \u2014 i.e., it is *managing* the risk. If \"granularity\" is read strictly as published geographic/product detail, no formal reduction has occurred for this report, making the risk \"emerging\" rather than realized.\n- **No Known Issue:** Not tenable.\n\n**Most appropriate level: High Risk**\n\nReading the rubric's High Risk trigger \u2014 \"reductions in granularity, timeliness, or frequency\" \u2014 the crop-estimating program has already sustained all three in some measure: **timeliness** (2025 reschedulings), **granularity** (permanent loss of cotton objective yield; congressionally-required restoration of county estimates; AFBF's on-record granularity concern), and **quality itself** (unprecedented revision, internal review, record-low response, market impact). A dataset whose errors move markets 5% and whose response rate is at an all-time low is not merely facing \"potential or emerging\" risk.\n\n**Counter-argument for Moderate Risk:** if the standard is confined to the *published* *Crop Production* artifact's cadence and geography, the report remains monthly, national/state/county, with no formal downgrade. That reading is defensible, but it understates that the underlying measurement system has measurably degraded. I therefore record **High Risk**.\n\n---"
