      "evidence": "**Evidence by risk level**\n\n- **Gone:** Not applicable \u2014 the program is active. The Federal Register notice of September 4, 2026 (91 FR 56835, signed by George Cook as *\"Deputy Under Secretary for Economic Affairs performing the non\u2011exclusive functions and duties of the Director of the Census Bureau\"*) states: *\"The most recent Economic Census was conducted in 2023 for the reference year 2022. The next Economic Census will occur in 2028 for the reference year 2027.\"*\n- **High Risk (arguments):**\n  - **The Economic Census's own OMB clearance appears to have lapsed without public renewal.** A Federal Register full\u2011text search on the control number **0607\u20110998** returns only three documents, the most recent dated **March 2, 2022**; a search for `\"2027 Economic Census\"` returns a single document (the Commodity Flow Survey notice). The 2022 ICR (202209\u20110607\u2011001) shows an **expiration date of 06/30/2025**. On a mechanical reading, that is an ICR expired more than a year with no published reinstatement.\n  - **Appropriations risk:** The House Appropriations Committee's FY2027 CJS bill (H.R. 8845, approved 32\u201328 on May 13, 2026) would fund the Census Bureau at **$1.49 billion \u2014 flat with FY2026 and ~$521 million below the Administration's $2.01 billion request**. Ranking Member DeLauro stated the level \"is very inadequate, **especially as the Bureau must ramp up efforts in fiscal year 2027 to prepare for the 2027 Economic Census**.\" With final bills unlikely before October 1, a continuing resolution is expected; The Census Project wrote to Secretary Lutnick on **July 29, 2026** asking for an FY2027 funding **anomaly** so that Economic Census (and 2030) planning isn't frozen at prior\u2011year rates.\n  - **Capacity warnings:** ASA's 2026 midyear report says staffing cuts and leadership vacancies \"put the agencies in a weakened and vulnerable position to meet rigorous production schedules.\"\n- **Moderate Risk:** The Administration's FY2027 request funds the next cycle explicitly: **2027 Economic Census \u2014 $166.9M BA/Obl., 420 FTE/509 positions, program change +$33.0M and +63 FTE/+80 positions**. The Commodity Flow Survey \u2014 described in its own ICR as \"**a component of the 2027 Economic Census**\" \u2014 is actively moving through OMB (60\u2011day comment closed July 20, 2026, Docket USBC\u20112026\u20110166). Nothing indicates a skipped collection or a termination; the uncertainty is whether the ramp\u2011up is funded and staffed enough to keep the standard schedule, and whether the EC\u2011specific clearance is filed in time.\n- **No Known Issue:** The 2022 cycle hit every announced milestone and finished early (a first). Statutory authority is intact. But \"no known issue\" overstates the ICR and appropriations picture.\n\n**Chosen level: Moderate Risk.**\nNothing shows a skipped or terminated census; the cycle is planned, named in the President's budget at a *higher* level, and one of its components is already in clearance. The risk is a delayed or resource\u2011truncated ramp\u2011up and \u2014 most concretely \u2014 the absence as of October 2026 of a published OMB clearance for the next Economic Census itself. **Escalation trigger:** if no 2027 Economic Census ICR notice appears by roughly mid\u20112027, or if FY2027 enacted funding lands at the House level with no anomaly, re\u2011rate this category **High**.\n\n---"
