      "evidence": "**Evidence by risk level**\n\n- **Gone:** No.\n- **High Risk (arguments):**\n  - **A named component of the 2027 Economic Census is losing content and sample.** The Commodity Flow Survey ICR (May 20, 2026) states the Bureau will **remove the Q1/Q4 hazardous\u2011materials packaging supplements**, cutting respondents from **160,000 (2022) to 140,000 (2027)**, alongside other methodological changes.\n  - **The annual companion program is losing detail and moving to modular subsampling.** The AIES ICR (January 20, 2026) proposes to **remove depreciable\u2011asset questions, selected capital\u2011expenditure details (finance leases, software), company\u2011level questions (leased employees, R&D spending), and selected revenue details**, and to \"implement a modular and subsampled content strategy.\"\n  - **The Bureau's own stated direction** (2026 \"ECON 101\" presentation) includes \"**Leverage External & Alternative Data \u2014 Explore alternative data sources to reduce reliance on survey collection**\" and \"Align content between Annual and Economic Census collections\" \u2014 i.e., the 2027 EC will not be a carbon copy of 2022.\n  - **Execution risk:** ~15\u201320% staff loss, a hiring freeze, and a Commerce contract\u2011approval backlog (>3,000 requests as of early May 2025 per the Washington Post, cited in ASA's report) all bear on a collection due to field in 2028. ASA found that cuts and policy changes \"led to certain public\u2011facing data sets being delayed, suspended or canceled.\"\n- **Moderate Risk:** These documented reductions sit in *adjacent/component* products (CFS 2027, AIES 2025\u201326), not yet in Economic Census\u2013proper content; the EC for 2027 has no published questionnaire or content plan I could locate, so the exposure is potential rather than realized. The 2022 cycle *improved* granularity and timeliness (accelerated release schedule; Geographic Area Statistics released simultaneously rather than piecemeal; new NAICS 115 agriculture\u2011support statistics; ~19 sectors, ~950 NAICS industries, ~21,000 geographies, ~7,900 products).\n- **No Known Issue:** True for the 2022 vintage, which was maintained and improved.\n\n**Chosen level: Moderate Risk.**\nI read the CFS/AIES changes as establishing a *direction of travel* \u2014 content consolidation and reduced reliance on survey collection \u2014 that has not yet been applied to the Economic Census core but plausibly will be, and that demonstrably reduces detail in the annual series users pair with the Census. A reviewer who treats the CFS as part of the Economic Census dataset could defend **High Risk**; I don't, because CFS retains its headline value/tons/mode/geography products and no EC\u2011core element has been dropped.\n\n---"
