      "evidence": "**Evidence for *Gone*:** None. Collection is active; a July 2026 release and press release exist.\n\n**Evidence for *High Risk*:** (1) HTOPS is explicitly an **experimental data product**, not a mandatory statistical series; the FY2026 Commerce budget justification directs the Bureau to eliminate functions \"not statutorily mandated or core to the Bureau's mission\" by end of FY2026 and consolidate \"duplicative\" areas. (2) **A prior collection was skipped** \u2014 no July 2025 wave, to allow \"operational updates to our systems and processes.\" (3) Clearance is granted in short increments (2-wave and 3-wave chunks), so continuation depends on repeated OMB approvals. (4) FY2027 justification shows **Current Demographic Statistics program change of \u2212$21.0M and \u221289 FTE / \u2212170 positions**, the account family that funds demographic survey modernization including this program.\n\n**Evidence for *Moderate Risk*:** (1) **Documented publication delays** \u2014 the December 2024 HPS content transitioned to national-only, and the March 2026 wave's data required a September 2026 re-release; Collection-to-PUF lags run ~1\u20133 months (e.g., February 2025 wave published May 6, 2025). (2) The 2025 **panel design failed and had to be abandoned**; Census now uses independent cross-sectional samples, a mid-course redesign indicating strain. (3) **~9\u201310% response rate** \u2014 the program's viability depends on weighting rather than participation. (4) FY2026 Current Surveys and Programs fell from $329M to $318.5M (\u22123%), and the FY2027 request proposes $288.5M (\u22129.4%), which the House Committee has so far accepted at $288.5M.\n\n**Evidence for *No Known Issue*:** OMB Control No. 0607\u20111029 is **Active**; the July/September/November 2026 non-substantive change was **approved without change on 09/01/2026**, with expiration shown as 09/30/2028. Collections occurred in March, May, and July 2026, and September/November 2026 were cleared. The FY2026 \u2192 FY2027 shift is also toward a *larger* HTOPS sample (\u2248136,000 housing units/wave), indicating real ongoing investment rather than drawdown.\n\n> **Assessment: Moderate Risk.** The ICR is active and renewed before expiration, and 2026 collections have proceeded, which rules out *High Risk* on the rubric's specific triggers (missed statutory deadline, skipped publication, ICR lapsed >1 year). But there is a documented on-the-record pattern of delays (weight-corrected re-release), a historically skipped wave, roughly 9.4% proposed cuts to the funding account, and an explicit policy of terminating non-statutorily-mandated functions. This is a discretionary program whose continuation is re-litigated every few months.\n\n---"
