      "evidence": "**Risk levels and evidence:**\n\n*Gone \u2014* No evidence. Historic releases remain on the Board's site; `federalreserve.gov/releases/G17/Hist/` and the 100-year accessible tables (1919\u20132018) are live. FRASER's G.17 title record (ID 77) holds scanned releases and the predecessor titles (E.5/G.3, G.12.2, G.12.3) back to 1915; FRED series `INDPRO` runs 1919-01 to 2026-08.\n\n*High Risk \u2014* No evidence any pre-current-year files have been removed.\n\n*Moderate Risk \u2014* No evidence any data *elements* have been dropped. However, note real **access-channel churn** (not data removal): the DDP \"View chart\" feature was retired Dec 18, 2025; \"Build Your Package\" is being removed the week of **Nov 9, 2026**; preformatted data packages are slated for removal later in 2026\u20132027 ahead of the **eventual retirement of the DDP**. The Board is publishing a **series-ID-to-FRED crosswalk** and retaining XML downloads on release pages, and all DDP series are mirrored in FRED/ALFRED. So the data survives, but user tooling and URL/ID conventions are changing.\n\n*No Known Issue \u2014* Best fit. Historical data remain publicly available with no known alteration. Preservation-relevant caveat: the upcoming **annual revision on Nov 24, 2026 rebases to 2022=100**, which will revise history from 1972 forward. That is routine revision, not removal \u2014 but the *current* 2017=100 vintage should be captured from ALFRED before that date.\n\n**Selected: No Known Issue.** Data files back to 1919 remain accessible; only delivery mechanisms are being consolidated into FRED. I flag the DDP retirement as an access-watch item, not a data-loss item.\n\n---"
