      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting \"No Known Issue\"**\n- **ICR is active and was renewed *before* expiry.** `reginfo.gov` OMB Control Number History for 2528-0029 shows ICR **202502-2528-004**, \"Extension without change of a currently approved collection,\" received by OIRA **03/06/2025**, **Conclusion: \"Approved without change,\" 04/15/2025** \u2014 clearing the prior 07/31/2025 expiration.\n- The Census respondent portal, fetched today, displays **\"OMB No.: 2528-0029 Approval Expires: 4/30/2028\"** and an active collection with **\"Due Date: October 19, 2026.\"** Collection is live.\n- **Publications are current:** latest data tables **September 2026**; annual data release **June 2026**; last ALFRED vintage **2026\u201110\u201106**.\n- Monthly cadence is otherwise unbroken: ALFRED vintages run monthly **2020\u201106\u201109 \u2192 2025\u201109\u201108**, and again monthly **2025\u201112\u201104 \u2192 2026\u201110\u201106**.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"Moderate Risk\"**\n- **Two consecutive monthly releases were missed.** The ALFRED vintage list jumps from **2025\u201109\u201108 to 2025\u201112\u201104** \u2014 i.e., **no releases in October or November 2025**. This coincides with the ~43-day federal government shutdown (Oct 1\u2013Nov 12, 2025), during which Census suspended data releases. \u2731 Relatedly, an industry blog notes the last \"U.S. Housing Completions Report\" before the lapse was published **10 October 2025**. \u2731\n- The underlying figures were **not lost**: FRED's shipments series contains values for **2025\u201110 (9.3)** and **2025\u201111 (7.2)**, so the data were published later (in the December 2025 release) rather than dropped.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"High Risk\"**\n- The rubric's literal wording \u2014 *\"collection or publication **skipped**\"* \u2014 is arguably met by the October and November 2025 release gaps. A strict reading would place this category at High Risk.\n- **Counterweight:** there is no *statutory* publication deadline for MHS (so that trigger is N/A), the ICR did **not** lapse (the \">1 year expired\" trigger is unmet), and there is **no loss of any monthly observation**. The disruption was external (government-wide shutdown), transient, and fully recovered.\n\n**\u27a1 Selected: Moderate Risk.** The ICR is healthy and collection/publication are current, so the durable-failure triggers are absent \u2014 but a *typical intended publication date was missed twice*, which is precisely the Moderate criterion. I read the rubric's \"publication skipped\" (High) as meaning a cycle whose data are never released; here the data were released late, so delay rather than skip is the accurate characterization. **If your convention treats a missing release event as a skip, escalate this category to High Risk.**\n\n---"
