      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting \"No Known Issue\"**\n- **Granularity has been maintained or improved:** the 2014 redesign and the **January 2021** form revision *added* content (leased-unit identification; placed vs. not-yet-placed distinction) and aligned characteristics with industry standards.\n- Products continue at full advertised resolution: **monthly** shipments/status/price by region, division, state and size; **annual** characteristics; annual **PUF** with weights and imputation flags (2025 file = 4,859 records).\n- Both **NSA and seasonally adjusted annual rate** shipments continue; rounding-in-first-release then unrounded-later convention is still documented; the April SA revision cycle is intact.\n- The external **IBTS/MHI state-level shipment releases** also continue (e.g., August 2025 state production/shipment tables). \u2731\n\n**Evidence supporting \"Moderate Risk\"**\n- **Demonstrated timeliness disruption:** the October\u2013November 2025 publication gap (see \u00a72).\n- **Collector capacity:** the Census Bureau lost **20% of its full-time positions and 33% of its senior staff** between January 2025 and July 2026 \u2731 \u2014 a material risk to editing, imputation, seasonal adjustment and disclosure-review capacity for a survey whose estimates already depend heavily on hot-deck and regression imputation.\n- **Emerging restriction on detail:** the new privacy directive \"will prevent Census from releasing data with as much detail as in the past\" and has already delayed detailed releases elsewhere. \u2731 (MHS is a *business* survey and has not yet visibly lost detail.)\n- **Sponsor reorganization:** HUD's withdrawn-but-expected-to-return reorganization moves PD&R's **Chief Data Officer** out of PD&R (to the CIO) and merges three PD&R divisions \u2014 a plausible risk to the sponsor-side analytical staffing behind the series.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"High Risk\"**\n- No documented **reduction** in MHS granularity, frequency or timeliness since the dataset's design. The 2025 gap was a one-off and recovered; nothing supports High.\n\n**\u27a1 Selected: Moderate Risk.** As of today granularity, frequency and content are intact (and were last *improved*, not reduced) \u2014 so not High. But the combination of a **demonstrated two-cycle timeliness gap**, a **20%/33% Census staffing loss**, a **detail-restricting privacy directive**, and **PD&R's pending reorganization** constitutes exactly a \"potential or emerging risk to granularity, timeliness, or frequency.\"\n\n---"
