    "statutory": {
      "level": "No Known Issue",
      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting \"No Known Issue\"**\n- **Authorization is explicitly named** in every current instrument: *\"Title 42 United States Code (U.S.C.), 5424 note; Title 13 U.S.C., Section 8(b); and Title 12 U.S.C., Section 1701z-1 authorize the Census Bureau to conduct this collection.\"* (respond.census.gov, current).\n- It is **required for the implementation of a federal program, not merely optional research:** HUD's Federal Register notice states **\"Section 2145(b) of the Housing and Economic Recovery Act (HERA) of 2008 requires HUD to develop a method of indexing to annually adjust Title I manufactured home loan limits. This index is partially based on manufactured housing price data collected by this survey.\"** HUD's **Final Rule \"Indexing Methodology for Title I Manufactured Home Loan Limits\" (published 28 February 2024)** established the methodologies, and revised limits applied \"for all manufactured home loans for which applications are received on or after **March 29, 2024**.\"\n- Concrete, named programmatic uses beyond loan limits: **fair market values and rents**, monitoring whether new homes are placed on **owned vs. rented lots**, monitoring **total housing production and affordability**, and **BEA's residential fixed-investment** component of GDP.\n- **No substitute collection exists:** the **AHS** measures the standing stock, not current monthly shipments/placements; Census's **New Residential Construction** explicitly **excludes HUD-Code manufactured homes**. Nothing else produces HUD-Code price/shipment/status data.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"Moderate Risk\"**\n- The core 1980-Act authority is phrased as **Secretary *discretion*** (\"provides the Secretary discretion to take necessary actions\u2026\") and is codified as a **statutory *note*** rather than an operative section. A maximalist reading could call that \"vague.\" However, **HERA \u00a72145(b)** supplies a hard, operative requirement that depends on MHS price data, which neutralizes the vagueness objection.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"High Risk\"**\n- None found. The only HUD PD&R items that lack alternatives are precisely the MHS-dependent ones; there is no evidence of MHS being declared duplicative or programmatically unused.\n\n**\u27a1 Selected: No Known Issue.** Authorization is explicitly named, its collection content is clear, it is **required for implementation of a federal program** (HERA-mandated Title I loan-limit indexing, plus FMRs/AMI-adjacent uses and GDP), and **no alternative data collection can substitute.** The discretionary framing of the 1980 Act is a drafting nuance, not a gap.\n\n---"
    },
