      "evidence": "*This category must be read against **both** the sponsor (HUD/PD&R) and the collector (Census Bureau).*\n\n**Evidence supporting \"High Risk\"**\n- **HUD (sponsor):** HUD's own reorganization notice: *\"Current staffing levels are almost **35 percent below** historic staffing levels,\"* with *\"a significant number of voluntary departures\"* since 2025 under the Deferred Resignation Program. Separate reporting: **\"an estimated 2,300 employees, or **23%** of the agency's staff, have voluntarily left\"** since January, followed by **RIF notices to hundreds more** (\u2248**442** terminations, \"last day\u2026 Dec. 9\"; ~114 Fair Housing, 103 Public & Indian Housing, 86 Office of Housing, 30 Community Planning and Development). \u2731\n- **Census Bureau (collector):** *\"From January 2025 [to] July 2026, Census Bureau lost **20 percent of its full-time positions, including 33 percent of its senior staff**.\"* \u2731 Taken with the Bureau's workforce size, **the \"1,000 or more staff lost\" High-Risk trigger is very likely met**, in addition to the \"\u226520\u201339% staff lost\" and \"leadership/senior-staff churn\" indicators.\n- **Leadership displacement:** the privacy directive \"came from political appointees **outside the agency**\"; HUD's reorganization realigns the **PD&R Chief Data Officer** to the CIO.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"Moderate Risk\"**\n- HUD: \"approximately **15%** of staff removed\" by mid-March 2025 through probationary terminations, accelerated retirements and an initial RIF \u2731 \u2014 i.e., the 10\u201339% band.\n- Census: the **Current Surveys and Programs** account was requested **\u201312.3%** in FY2026 \u2731; the enacted FY2026 Census total (**$1.49B**) was a **7.8% increase** over FY2025 ($1.38B), with $318.5M for Current Surveys and Programs \u2731 \u2014 so **no 20% budget cut and no elimination of the account**.\n- HUD's own notice is explicit that the reorganization \"includes **no steps to relocate or reduce any staff**\" and that PD&R changes are consolidations \"rather than any change in terms of\u2026 staffing levels.\"\n\n**Evidence supporting \"No Known Issue\"**\n- Neither agency lost all staff or all funding; MHS collection, processing and publication are demonstrably functioning.\n\n**\u27a1 Selected: High Risk.** The trigger is \"40% or more of staff lost, **and/or** 1,000 or more staff lost, **and/or** budget cut \u226520%, **and/or** leadership removed.\" HUD\u2014the sponsor\u2014is roughly **23\u201335% below** historic staffing with a further RIF, and Census\u2014the collector\u2014has lost **20% of FTE and 33% of senior staff**, which almost certainly exceeds 1,000 positions. Even if the percentage tests are argued, the **absolute-headcount trigger is satisfied at both agencies**, and leadership/senior-staff churn is documented. The mitigating factor (Census's FY2026 appropriation was not cut by 20%) does not offset the personnel triggers.\n\n---"
