      "evidence": "**Gone / High / Moderate \u2014 supporting evidence**\n- A legacy \"Monthly Historical Data\" shell (`census.gov/wholesale/www/historic_releases/monthly_historic_releases.html`) renders with **no data content** even though it carries a 2026 \"last revised\" stamp. This is a **broken/moved asset, not an agency removal** \u2014 the same materials are served from the current Historical Data and Time Series pages. Treat it as a cataloging hazard (dead page at a canonical-looking URL), not data loss.\n- The **annual benchmark/revision report is delayed**: Census's own \"Special Notice\" on every 2026 release states that because AWTS transitioned to the **Annual Integrated Economic Survey (AIES)**, \"the Annual Revision Report for the Monthly Wholesale Trade Survey (MWTS) will be delayed.\" That means **prior\u2011year estimates remain unrevised longer than normal**, and the current\u2011cycle annual product is missing. This is a *timeliness/revision* gap affecting older data, not a deletion.\n- The Senate Appropriations Committee report accompanying the FY2026 CJS bill directs the Census Bureau to report on **\"the removal of Census and BEA data sets\"** and the termination of Census advisory committees (CRS IF13105, updated Mar. 19, 2026) \u2014 i.e., Congress itself is monitoring removals across the agency. No MWTS-specific removal is documented.\n\n**No Known Issue \u2014 supporting evidence**\n- The **Historical Monthly Wholesale Trade Reports** page is live with year tabs **2026 back through 2001** (PDF + Excel per month), and a decade-by-decade archive reaching the **1936\u20131939** bundle; the **Time Series** page carries SIC-era files (sales 1967\u20132001; inventories/I\u2011S 1980\u20132001) and SIC\u2194NAICS concordances.\n- **Restricted-use MWTS microdata** are still listed as available for **2006\u20132024** (Census economic microdata index).\n- Independent vintages remain intact on **FRED release 290 / ALFRED** and the Census API, giving redundant copies of historical series.\n\n**Most appropriate: No Known Issue.**\nThe pre\u20112026 archive is still publicly reachable in PDF, Excel and time-series form, and nothing indicates files were pulled. The caveats (one dead legacy page; a delayed annual revision that leaves older estimates unrefreshed; congressional scrutiny of \"data set removal\" generally) are real but are process/timeliness problems rather than loss of historical access.\n\n---"
