      "evidence": "**Gone** \u2014 n/a.\n\n**High Risk \u2014 supporting evidence (weak)**\n- The authority is **broad, not program-specific**: \"**Title 13 U.S.C. 131 and 182**\" authorizes the survey (FR notice), and response is **voluntary** \u2014 so nothing in statute compels either collection or continued publication.\n- Practical substitutes have been improvised during disruptions (BEA/Census and private sources filled gaps during the 2025 data blackout), and BEA could interpolate wholesale inventories from annual benchmarks if MWTS ended.\n\n**Moderate Risk \u2014 supporting evidence (defensible)**\n- MWTS is **not named in statute**; it is authorized under general statistical authority, yet it is **required for the implementation of a federal program** \u2014 BEA's GDP, GDP-by-industry, input\u2013output accounts and the **inventory valuation adjustments applied to GDP** \u2014 and the **BLS uses it for PPI and productivity** inputs (FR 2025\u201115462).\n- There are **no alternative collections** that serve as substitutes for current monthly wholesale sales/inventories; Census calls MWTS \"the only source of current data on wholesale trade.\"\n\n**No Known Issue \u2014 supporting evidence (best fit)**\n- **Statutory authorization is explicitly named** (13 U.S.C. 131, 182), the content is defined by OMB-approved instruments (**SM4217\u2011A / SM4217\u2011E**, control 0607\u20110190), and a **current clearance exists**.\n- It is a **designated Principal Federal Economic Indicator**, with **no substitute source** and documented **programmatic federal use** (BEA, BLS, Fed, CEA, Treasury).\n\n**Most appropriate: No Known Issue,** with a caveat. Explicit Title 13 authorization + unique content + named federal program use + no substitutes matches \"No Known Issue.\" The honest counterpoint is that Title 13 \u00a7\u00a7131/182 confer *permission*, not a *requirement*, and response is voluntary \u2014 the Moderate definition above, which is why a skeptical reading is available.\n\n---"
