      "evidence": "**Gone** \u2014 No.\n\n**High Risk \u2014 supporting evidence**\n- ASA's *The Nation's Data at Risk* reports the Census Bureau **lost an estimated 15% of its staffing since FY24**. Using the FY2026 request's own baseline (7,657 = a 9% decline from FY24 \u2248 8,414), that implies roughly **~1,200+ positions** \u2014 which meets the rubric's \"**1,000 or more staff lost**\" trigger *if* the 15% applies to total Bureau staffing. (This is the estimate most likely to drive a High Risk call.)\n- Government Executive (July 29, 2026): \"**All 13 principal federal statistical agencies have seen staffing cuts**\u2026 **Six of them shed at least a third of their headcount**, and two lost more than two-thirds,\" with Census among the agencies \"hard hit\"; ASA's 2026 midyear report warns cuts plus \"**persistent leadership vacancies**\" weaken the ability to meet production schedules.\n- Commerce inspector general found the Bureau \"**lacks effective strategies to address staffing gaps and high vacancies**\" in field representative positions.\n- **Two funding lapses** (Oct 1\u2013Nov 12, 2025; early 2026) halted collection and publication; the FY2026 *request* proposed cutting the Current Surveys and Programs account **12.3%** ($329M \u2192 $289M).\n\n**Moderate Risk \u2014 supporting evidence**\n- The **percentage** metric is 10\u201339% (\u224815% as of mid-2025), not \u226540%.\n- **Enacted funding went up, not down**: P.L. 119\u201174 provides Census **$1.490B in FY2026**, **$108M above** the FY2024/FY2025 enacted $1.383B; the Current Surveys and Programs account is **$319M**, only **$10M (~3%) below** FY2024/25.\n- **Current Economic Statistics \u2014 the account that contains MWTS \u2014 was requested and funded essentially flat at $216M** (FY2024, FY2025, FY2026 request all $216M).\n- The lapses were temporary; Commerce/Census data resumed, and hiring has resumed at several statistical agencies (ASA, 2026).\n\n**No Known Issue** \u2014 Not supportable: ~15% headcount loss and reported vacancies exceed the <10% threshold.\n\n**Most appropriate: High Risk.** The percentage reading sits in the Moderate band, but the rubric's disjunctive test (\"1,000 or more staff lost\") is plausibly satisfied by an estimated ~1,200 positions, and the qualitative evidence (agency described as \"hard hit,\" IG findings on vacancies, ASA warnings about production schedules, two lapses in one year) supports the higher grade. **If you prefer a percentage-only rule, downgrade to Moderate** \u2014 that is the key sensitivity in this rubric.\n\n---"
