      "evidence": "**Evidence by level**\n\n- **No Known Issue:** NHIS continues to be fielded and published. The 2025 files were released (Sept. 30, 2026); 2026 collection is underway; 2027 is planned with detailed sampling/questionnaires in the ICR. The **ICR is active and being renewed**: OMB No. 0920\u20110214 expires 12/31/2026 and an extension for **2027\u20132029** was noticed Aug. 20, 2026 \u2014 i.e., renewed before expiration.\n- **Moderate Risk (typical publication date missed / collection delayed):** The 2025 field period was **paused for ~6 weeks** by the appropriations lapse; NCHS itself notes \"slight delays in the processing of data.\" The **2025 full-year public-use file appeared Sept. 30, 2026**, versus the customary ~June\u2013July release (SHADAC's tracker lists NHIS's normal release window as June and marked the 2024 release \"on time\"). Quarterly early-release estimates were **discontinued in favor of biannual releases**. Also, **NHIS-Teen was not conducted in 2026**.\n- **High Risk:** No statutory deadline missed; no cycle skipped; ICR not expired (it is being renewed).\n- **Gone:** Not terminated.\n\n**Most appropriate: Moderate Risk.**\nCollection and publication both continued, and the ICR is being renewed on time, so nothing rises to High. But the 2025 collection was genuinely interrupted and the 2025 public-use file landed roughly a quarter later than the recent norm, matching the rubric's \"collection or publication delayed / typical publication date missed.\" The sub-product pause (NHIS-Teen) slightly reinforces this.\n\n---"
