      "evidence": "**Evidence by level**\n\n- **No Known Issue (statutorily required, clear content, no full substitutes):** NHIS rests on explicit statutory authority \u2014 the **National Health Survey Act of 1956** (P.L. 84\u2011652) and **\u00a7306 of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 242k)**, which direct NCHS to collect national statistics on illness, disability, health-services use, costs, and related matters, and to improve survey methods. Confidentially protected under PHSA \u00a7308(d)/CIPSEA. NHIS has documented programmatic uses (Healthy People 2030 monitoring; insurance/access measurement; ACA evaluation). NCHS's own 2028 ICR says content decisions will **prioritize \"topics that are consistent with NCHS' legislative requirements.\"**\n- **Moderate Risk:** Doesn't fit \u2014 NHIS *is* explicitly required, not merely program-implementing.\n- **High Risk:** Vague authorization + substitutes + no programmatic use \u2014 none apply. (There are *partial* substitutes \u2014 BRFSS, MEPS, NHANES, CPS ASEC, ACS cover some overlapping topics such as insurance or disability \u2014 but no single collection replaces NHIS.)\n\n**Most appropriate: No Known Issue.**\nNHIS is squarely and explicitly mandated, its required content is identifiable, its uses are programmatically real, and there is no full substitute. The existence of partial topic overlaps is not sufficient to trigger High Risk.\n\n---"
