      "evidence": "**Evidence by level**\n\n- **No Known Issue:** FY25 NCHS budget was held flat at **$187.4M**; both appropriations committees proposed maintaining **$187.397M** for FY26; **all Oct. 2025 NCHS RIFs were rescinded** by Nov. 18, 2025; AP News reported NCHS \"has been barely touched in layoffs, and seems intact under current budget plans.\"\n- **Moderate Risk (10\u201339% staff lost / 10\u201319% budget cut / threatened leadership change):**\n  - Amstat's \"The Nation's Data at Risk 2025\" reports **NCHS had lost an estimated 10% of staff by Fall 2025** \u2014 at the Moderate threshold.\n  - During the shutdown, **~110 NCHS employees were RIF'd** (~25% of the agency per AJPH), including National Death Index staff and legal/statistical infrastructure staff; notices were later **rescinded**.\n  - The **FY26 President's Budget Request proposed $175M (a ~9% cut)** and a **reorganization moving NCHS out of CDC** into an HHS \"Office of Strategy\" \u2014 a **threatened change in leadership/organization**.\n  - A temporary suspension on RIF actions was scheduled to lift **Jan. 31, 2026**, leaving further reductions possible.\n- **High Risk (\u226540% staff, \u22651,000 staff, \u226520% budget cut, or leadership removed):** The Oct. 2025 RIFs approached this scale (~25%) but were reversed; budget cuts proposed (~9%) remain under the 20% line; leadership has not been removed.\n- **Gone:** No.\n\n**Most appropriate: Moderate Risk.**\nThe measured **~10% staffing loss** sits at the Moderate threshold, and there is a **credible threatened organizational/leadership change** (the proposed move out of CDC) plus a proposed budget reduction and demonstrated RIF volatility. Because none of the High-Risk thresholds (\u226540% staff lost, \u226520% budget cut, leadership removed) are currently met, Moderate is the best fit.\n\n---"
