      "evidence": "**Evidence by level**\n\n- **No Known Issue:** NHIS's own pages show no negative policy note and no termination; the survey continues normally.\n- **Moderate Risk (proposed/pending changes; official statements of planned change):** The **2028 redesign** is the central NHIS policy development, and it is formally **proposed and pending** \u2014 a Federal Register notice (Aug. 20, 2026), draft questionnaires, a public comment period through **Oct. 20, 2026**, and OMB approval not sought until 2027. The **proposed NCHS reorganization** and FY26 budget cut are likewise proposals, not enacted.\n- **High Risk (Presidential-Action-driven ICR; negative policy note on site; other significant changes per Administration priorities):**\n  - CDC's website and data holdings were **modified \"to comply with President Trump's Executive Orders\"** (Defending Women from Gender Ideology; Ending DEI Programs) \u2014 the agency carried an explicit compliance note and **removed 203 datasets (13%)** in early 2025, with content scrubbed/altered (NPR; Univ. of Michigan Law). This is \"other significant changes in accordance with Administration priorities\" at the *publisher*.\n  - The **Oct. 2025 shutdown-era RIF of ~110 NCHS staff** was widely characterized as politically driven and included data-integrity and NDI staff (AJPH).\n  - The **cost-driven 2028 redesign** (content cuts, mode shift) and the **Administration's proposed NCHS reorganization/budget cut** align with the Administration's efficiency/cost-cutting priorities.\n  - HHS's Healthy People site (a key NHIS consumer) carries a **court-ordered restoration notice** with overt Administration policy rhetoric.\n- **Gone:** No termination.\n\n**Most appropriate: High Risk.**\nAt least one High-Risk trigger is clearly satisfied \u2014 the **EO-driven, agency-wide modification/removal of CDC data**, which has already altered federal health data and is explicitly stated to be in accordance with Administration priorities. Added to the shutdown-era NCHS RIF, the Administration-backed NCHS reorganization proposal, and a redesign that NCHS itself frames around cost reduction and that will \"substantially\" cut content, the policy environment for the publisher is materially adverse. I note the honest counter-argument that **NHIS's own instrument changes remain proposed/pending** (which is textbook Moderate) and that NHIS's pages carry no negative note \u2014 so a defensible alternative reading is Moderate. On balance, the demonstrated Administration-driven changes at the publishing agency push this to High.\n\n---"
