      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting \"Gone\"**\n- Neither HUD's Office of Policy Development and Research (PD&R) nor the Census Bureau has been eliminated; both continue to operate and both released the 2024 RHFS in February 2026.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"High Risk\" (\u226540% staff lost, and/or \u22651,000 staff lost, and/or \u226520% budget cut, and/or leadership removed)**\n- **Headcount:** HUD's FY2026 Congressional Justification states the budget is built on a 2025 reduction of **~1,750 staff** from the 2024 end-of-year level, and that \"current staffing levels are **almost 35 percent below historic staffing levels**.\" Independent reporting described HUD as facing \"sweeping layoffs, with potentially up to half of its total workforce to be eliminated,\" with ~15% already gone by mid-March 2025 and reductions effected largely through two rounds of the Deferred Resignation Program.\n- **Funding:** The FY2026 President's Budget requested **$95 million** for PD&R's **Research and Technology** account \u2014 **$44 million (32%) below** the FY2025 enacted level of $139 million \u2014 the account that funds PD&R surveys and data infrastructure. The Senate committee bill proposed a **$16.3 million (33%) cut** to the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Research and Technology; the House committee proposed a 10% reduction.\n- **Leadership:** HUD's own Federal Register notices are signed by a \"Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary\" (Sept. 2025) and a \"General Deputy Assistant Secretary for Policy Development and Research\" (Sept. 2026), indicating no confirmed Assistant Secretary for PD&R across the period. A department-wide reorganization proposing to merge three PD&R divisions into one and realign the Chief Data Officer to the CIO was circulated and then withdrawn, with implementation expected across FY2026\u2013FY2027. HUD also underwent a change of Secretary in 2025.\n- **Partner agency:** Census Bureau staffing is **down an estimated ~15% since FY2024**; the FY2026 request cut the **Current Surveys and Programs** account by ~$40 million (12.3%), and Government Executive (July 2026) reported statistical agencies \"stretched thin\"; the American Statistical Association's 2026 mid-year update documents \"substantially fewer staff, reduced purchasing power, and significant leadership gaps.\"\n\n**Evidence supporting \"Moderate Risk\" (10\u201339% staff lost, 500\u2013999 lost, 10\u201319% budget cut)**\n- Congress ultimately **reversed much of the proposed damage**: the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2026 (P.L. 119-75, Feb. 3, 2026) gave HUD a **12.6% increase** in net discretionary funding, and the enacted Research and Technology level came in **above** the President's request and above both committee proposals (CRS reported the final level as materially higher than FY2025; Senate and House committee drafts had proposed \u221233% and \u221210%). A $20 million rescission to R&T was also proposed in the House bill but is not reflected in the enacted figure.\n\n**Evidence supporting \"No Known Issue\"**\n- None. Neither staffing nor budget has been held flat.\n\n**\u27a1 Most appropriate risk level: High Risk.**\nThe rubric's triggers are met on multiple independent grounds: **more than 1,000 staff lost** (\u22481,750 at HUD in 2025, with HUD ~35% below historic levels), a **proposed budget cut well in excess of 20%** to the specific account that pays for the survey, and **leadership churn** (no confirmed PD&R Assistant Secretary; reorganization plans circulated and withdrawn). Congressional restoration of top-line HUD funding mitigates but does not offset the loss of institutional knowledge and analytical capacity in PD&R and at Census.\n\n---"
