      "evidence": "**Evidence supporting each level**\n\n- **Gone:** No.\n- **High Risk (two criteria met):**\n  - **Staff losses exceed 1,000 and ~20%.** OPM total employees at Census: **FY23 15,348 \u2192 FY24 13,232 \u2192 FY25 11,288 \u2192 FY26 10,683** \u2014 a **19.3% decline from FY24 to FY26 (~2,549 staff)** and **~30% from FY23 (~4,665 staff)** (ASA *2026 Midyear Update: Resources and Leadership Supporting Federal Statistics*). The proposed-plan figures are even steeper (8,565 \u2192 7,183 FTE).\n  - **Leadership removed/changed.** Director **Robert Santos resigned Jan 30, 2025**; the position remains **vacant** (no Senate-confirmed director); a **political appointee (George Cook) performs the duties of director**; the **chief scientist / associate director for research and methodology is vacant or acting**; **18 of 43 division heads are acting**; the **director vacancy is a live controversy** (NPR/AP 2025\u20132026; The Federalist, Sep 4, 2026).\n- **Moderate Risk (proportional view):** By percentage alone (10\u201339%), Census's loss sits in the Moderate band; the FY2026 **Current Surveys and Programs** appropriation of **$319M was only ~3% below** FY2024/25's $329M, and the **Current Economic Statistics** request was **flat at $216M** \u2014 i.e., the SOC's immediate account was not cut by 10\u201320%.\n- **No Known Issue:** No.\n\n**Selected level: High Risk.** Although the SOC's funding line is roughly flat and its own division has not been reported as decimated, the rubric is explicit that **\"1,000 or more staff lost\"** and/or **\"leadership removed\"** triggers High Risk, and Census meets both agency-wide. The FY2027 budget request also proposes **further substantial reductions for statistical agencies** (2030 Census being the main exception), reinforcing the direction of travel.\n\n---"
