      "evidence": "**Evidence \u2014 Gone**\n\n- No evidence. Archived news releases remain posted back to 1993; underlying index history dates to 1974/1983 and remains in the LABSTAT database and the FTP flat files (`download.bls.gov/pub/time.series/ei/`).\n\n**Evidence \u2014 High Risk**\n\n- **Supplementary data products were removed from the live site.** Effective September 13, 2024, BLS ceased publishing the MXP **History Tables, Index and Percent Change Tables, and Percent Change Analysis Tables**; only the Percent Change and Historical Reference Table remains. These are, literally, pre-existing data files withdrawn from the primary location.\n- **Annual series retirements.** Each year BLS changes publication status of individual indexes; with the **January 2026 indexes (released March 5, 2026), 35 indexes were introduced and 67 were discontinued**, the discontinued ones ending with **December 2025**. Over time this erodes the set of long, continuous histories.\n- **Base-period churn** (2000=100 \u2192 January 2025=100 for the administrative-data series \u2192 December 2025=100 for 35 new 2026 series; five indexes rebased to Dec. 2025=100 starting April 2026) makes historical comparisons harder for automated pipelines, though it does not delete data.\n\n**Evidence \u2014 Moderate Risk**\n\n- **A permanent hole was cut into prior-period data.** October 2025 survey data could not be collected because of the federal appropriations lapse; BLS states it was **\"unable to retroactively collect these data.\"** For the October 2025 reference period, **864 import and export price indexes \u2014 including the all-goods import and export indexes \u2014 are \"permanently suppressed from publication\"** and are not in the BLS database. The October 2025 news release is likewise listed as **\"Not published because of 2025 lapse in federal government appropriations.\"**\n- Discontinued series histories generally remain queryable in the database (`ei.series` records begin/end periods rather than deleting data), so the loss is of *specific data elements and products*, not wholesale archives.\n\n**Evidence \u2014 No Known Issue**\n\n- Core time series prior to 2026 remain accessible via the database, the API, FTP flat files, and FRED/ALFRED mirrors; news release archive is intact back to 1993.\n\n**Determination: Moderate Risk.** The dataset's historical backbone is intact, but discrete prior-period data elements have been removed: the September 2024 retirement of three published table products and the permanent suppression of October 2025 values across 864 series (including the headline all-goods indexes). This matches \"some data elements that exist in the dataset prior to the current year or cycle are removed.\" A strict reading of the rubric's High Risk (\"some data files prior to the current year are removed\") could be defended on the table retirements plus the Oct. 2025 hole, but no wholesale removal of historical files has occurred.\n\n---"
